    "result": "I implemented `compare_versions` in `solution.py` and wrote 85 pytest tests in `tests/test_solution.py`. The suite passes with `python -m pytest -q tests` (85 passed). The parser validates each part against the spec (no leading zeros on core and numeric prerelease identifiers, ASCII-only identifiers, no empty identifiers) and raises `ValueError` for anything else, including non-string input. Precedence is handled by building a comparable key per version: a release outranks any prerelease with the same core, and prerelease identifiers compare numerically when numeric, as ASCII strings otherwise, with numeric ranked lower and shorter lists lower. `BUILD` is validated but excluded from the key. I did not run coverage, CRAP, mutation or bandit checks, since only the test command was allowed, so those gates are unverified. By reading the code, every line should be reached by the tests, and no function is complex enough to have a high CRAP score.",
